WEBVTT

00:00:00.026 --> 00:00:03.907
A practical RAG system needs a small evaluation set.

00:00:03.907 --> 00:00:08.807
For each question, we record the document that should contain the supporting evidence.

00:00:08.807 --> 00:00:16.267
Our examples include payment plans, internship registration, scholarship appeals, VPN troubleshooting,

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.837
and other topics from the knowledge base.

00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:25.082
These expected document IDs let us evaluate retrieval independently from generation.

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:31.222
This is important because a final answer can fail for different reasons.

00:00:31.222 --> 00:00:35.882
[tts-break] Maybe the retriever never found the right source.

00:00:35.882 --> 00:00:39.742
Maybe the right source was present but the prompt was weak.

00:00:39.742 --> 00:00:42.752
Maybe the language model ignored the evidence.

00:00:42.752 --> 00:00:47.137
If we measure retrieval first, we can separate those problems.

00:00:47.137 --> 00:00:52.792
Start with a small set of questions that you understand well.

00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:57.892
Include easy examples, paraphrases, exact-term queries, and a few difficult cases.

00:00:57.892 --> 00:01:03.190
As the system grows, expand the evaluation set with real user questions.
